---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { CLOCKS } from '../data/clocks';
import { LICENSES } from '../data/licenses';
import { STATES } from '../data/states';
import { SITE } from '../data/site';

const pairCount = STATES.length * LICENSES.length;
---

<Layout
  title="Methods — FastLicense"
  description="How FastLicense treats statutory clocks, regulator-published times, and unknown typical ranges."
>
  <section class="masthead">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">Methods</p>
      <h1>Unknown is a finding, not a gap we fill.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        {pairCount} state × license pages. {CLOCKS.length} clocks with a statute or a
        regulator-published timeframe as of {SITE.accessed}.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>1. Three kinds of time</h2>
      <p><strong>Typical processing time.</strong> A range the regulator itself publishes (“reviewed within 3 weeks”). Rare for company licenses. If we cannot point at that page, the label is “Typical range unknown.”</p>
      <p><strong>Statutory decision clock.</strong> A statute that says the agency must approve or deny within N days after a defined start date. That is a legal outer bound, not an average. Completeness rules and “good cause” extensions matter.</p>
      <p><strong>Deemed approval.</strong> Some money-transmitter statutes treat silence after the clock as approval. New York’s money-transmitter statute does not. We only mark deemed approval where the cited section says so.</p>

      <h2>2. What we will not print</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Invented “fast-track” and “standard” week ranges.</li>
        <li>Vendor price sheets (that is <a href={SITE.sisters.store.url}>License Store</a>).</li>
        <li>Who must file, or which form to use (that is <a href={SITE.sisters.simple.url}>Licensing Made Simple</a>).</li>
        <li>Star ratings, testimonials, or unnamed “clients say.”</li>
        <li>A hometown slant. The index is fifty states, same template.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>3. NMLS is a channel, not a timer</h2>
      <p>
        The Nationwide Multistate Licensing System is how many of these company licenses are
        requested. NMLS does not replace the state review clock. Confirm the current license name
        and checklist in the NMLS Checklist Compiler before you treat a page as the filing path.
      </p>

      <h2>4. Adjacent facts</h2>
      <p>
        Individual mortgage loan originator endorsements sometimes have a published window (see
        California DRE on the California mortgage page). That is labeled as an MLO clock, not as
        the company-license typical time.
      </p>

      <h2>5. Refresh</h2>
      <p>
        Sources on this build are stamped accessed {SITE.accessed}. Statutes and DRE timeframes
        move. Follow the URL on the page before you promise a launch date.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
